\section{Drive and decay near the collapsed solution}
\label{sec:theory}

We analyze the gradient flow of \eqref{eq:loss} for a linear encoder and predictor, which is exact for linear JEPAs and describes deep JEPAs to the extent that their early training is linear around initialization (\cref{sec:discussion}).
Time $t$ is measured in units of the encoder learning rate times steps; the predictor learns $\alpha$ times faster; weight decay is $\lambda$ on the encoder and $\lambda_P$ on the predictor.
Proofs are in \cref{app:proofs}.

\subsection{Directions of representation space}

\begin{assumption}[Shared directions]
\label{ass:codiag}
$\Ccc$ and $\Ctc$ are symmetric and share an orthonormal eigenbasis $\{u_j\}$: $\Ccc u_j=c_ju_j$, $\Ctc u_j=s_ju_j$.
\end{assumption}
$c_j$ is the context variance along direction $j$, $s_j$ the covariance between context and target along it, and $\rho_j=s_j/c_j$ the regression coefficient of the target on the context: how predictable direction $j$ of the target is.
\Cref{ass:codiag} holds whenever the two views are exchangeable and the input is stationary under the masking, e.g.\ for random masking of a stationary signal (\cref{prop:masking}).

\begin{proposition}[Decoupling; cf.\ \citealp{tian2021understanding}]
\label{prop:decouple}
Under \cref{ass:codiag}, if $W(0)=\sum_j \varepsilon_j v_ju_j^\top$ and $P(0)=\sum_j p_{0} v_jv_j^\top$ for orthonormal $\{v_j\}$, then $W(t)=\sum_j w_j(t)v_ju_j^\top$, $P(t)=\sum_jp_j(t)v_jv_j^\top$ for all $t$, where, with a stop-gradient target ($\Wt=W$),
\begin{align}
\dot w_j &= w_j\big(\underbrace{p_js_j}_{\text{drive } \drive_j}-\underbrace{(p_j^2c_j+\lambda)}_{\text{decay } \decay_j}\big), \label{eq:wdot}\\
\dot p_j &= \alpha\big(w_j^2(s_j-p_jc_j)-\lambda_Pp_j\big). \label{eq:pdot}
\end{align}
\end{proposition}
Each direction is a two-dimensional system, independent of all others.
With $s_j=1$, $c_j=1+\sigma^2$, $\lambda=\alpha\lambda_P$ equal to the weight decay and $w_j^2$ in place of their $s_j$, \eqref{eq:wdot}--\eqref{eq:pdot} are the SimSiam eigenmode equations of \citet{tian2021understanding}; what changes for JEPAs is that $s_j$ and $c_j$ are the context--target covariances induced by the masking (\cref{prop:masking}), so they differ across directions and with the masking ratio.
The amplitude $w_j$ grows at rate $r_j(p)=\drive_j(p)-\decay_j(p)$: the driving force $p s_j$ is the part of the gradient that pulls the prediction towards the predictable part of the target; the decay $p^2c_j$ is the predictor's amplification of context variance, to which weight decay adds $\lambda$.
For general initializations the decoupling holds approximately while $W$ is small; we test its accuracy for full-matrix training in \cref{sec:experiments}.

\subsection{Collapse is decided near the collapsed solution}

At the collapsed solution $w_j=0$, the predictor receives no gradient signal: \eqref{eq:pdot} is $O(w_j^2)$, and $p_j$ only decays, $p_j(t)\approx p_0e^{-\alpha\lambda_Pt}$.
Meanwhile direction $j$ grows if and only if $r_j(p_j)>0$.

\begin{proposition}[Growth window and bistability; cf.\ \citealp{tian2021understanding}]
\label{prop:window}
$r_j(p)>0$ if and only if $p\in(p_j^-,p_j^+)$, the roots of $c_jp^2-s_jp+\lambda=0$; the window is nonempty if and only if
\begin{equation}
s_j^2/c_j=\rho_j s_j>4\lambda.
\label{eq:fp}
\end{equation}
For $\lambda,\lambda_P>0$ the collapsed point $(w_j,p_j)=(0,0)$ is locally stable. A non-collapsed fixed point exists if and only if \eqref{eq:fp} holds, at $p_j^\ast=p_j^+$, $w_j^{\ast2}=\lambda_Pp_j^+/(s_j-p_j^+c_j)$, and it is locally stable.
\end{proposition}
Thus every direction is bistable: whether it ends at the collapsed point or at the non-collapsed one is decided by where the trajectory goes from its initial condition $(\varepsilon_j,p_0)$, i.e.\ early, while $w_j$ is still small.
Condition \eqref{eq:fp} and the bistability are the per-direction form of the fixed-point analysis of \citet{tian2021understanding}; what is missing is which basin a trajectory from a small initialization ends in, which is what determines the rank.
The number of directions satisfying \eqref{eq:fp} bounds the rank, but the bound is not tight: a direction can satisfy it and still collapse, because the predictor drifts out of the window before $w_j$ is large enough to hold it there.

\begin{proposition}[Early-time survival criterion, leading order]
\label{prop:survival}
Assume $\varepsilon_j\ll1$, $\lambda_P\lesssim\lambda$ and $\alpha\lesssim1$ (the regime is discussed in \cref{app:survival}), and let $p_T=p_0e^{-\alpha\lambda_PT}$.
To leading order in a two-phase (matched) approximation, direction $j$ survives a training time $T$ if and only if \eqref{eq:fp} holds and
\begin{equation}
\Phi_j=\max_{p_T<p<\min(p_0,\rho_j)}
\Big[\log\varepsilon_j+G_j(p)-\tfrac12\log\frac{\lambda_Pp}{s_j-pc_j}\Big]>0,
\label{eq:survival}
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
G_j(p)=\frac{s_j(p_0-p)-\tfrac{c_j}{2}(p_0^2-p^2)-\lambda\log\frac{p_0}{p}}{\alpha\lambda_P}.
\label{eq:G}
\end{equation}
The predicted rank is $\hat R=\#\{j:\Phi_j>0,\ \rho_js_j>4\lambda\}$.
\end{proposition}
$G_j(p)$ is the log-amplitude a direction gains while the predictor drifts down from $p_0$ to $p$ (the integral of drive minus decay); the last term is the log-amplitude it needs to cross the predictor's nullcline, above which the predictor starts learning from that direction and the trajectory is captured by the non-collapsed fixed point.
\Cref{prop:survival} is an asymptotic statement, not a theorem; \cref{app:survival} derives it, lists where it fails, and checks it against the integrated mode dynamics (297 of 300 random settings, against 183 for \eqref{eq:fp} alone), which are checks of the derivation, not results about trained JEPAs.

\Cref{fig:mechanism} illustrates the mechanism.
The decisive fact is that near collapse the predictor learns nothing and only decays: the window $(p^-_j,p^+_j)$ is open for a finite time, and a direction survives only if it grows enough during that time.
The threshold on predictability is therefore set by the early dynamics---by $\varepsilon_j$, $p_0$, $\alpha$, $\lambda_P$---and not only by the fixed-point condition.

