\paragraph{Weight decay.}
$\lambda$ enters only the decay: it lowers every rate by the same amount, narrows every window and closes it at $\rho_js_j=4\lambda$ (\cref{prop:window}), and lowers every $\Phi_j$; the predictor's weight decay sets how fast the window closes.
A direction with no drive decays at the rate $p_j^2c_j-p_js_j+\lambda$, which is exactly $\lambda$ outside the predictor's range, so the decay term can be calibrated against the weight decay independently of the drive: the log-eigenvalue of the embedding covariance along such a direction falls at rate $2\lambda$ per unit time.
At $\lambda=\lambda_P=0$ no window closes, every direction with $s_j>0$ and $p_0<\rho_j$ grows, and the predictor converges to $\rho_j$ along it (\cref{app:prior}).

\paragraph{Stop-gradient.}
\begin{proposition}[cf.\ Theorem~2 of \citealp{tian2021understanding}]
\label{prop:stopgrad}
Without the stop-gradient (gradients flow through the target branch, $\Wt=W$), $\dot w_j=-w_j\big(c_j(p_j-\rho_j)^2+c^t_j-\rho_js_j+\lambda\big)$, where $c^t_j=u_j^\top\Ctt u_j\ge\rho_js_j$ by Cauchy--Schwarz; every direction decays for every predictor.
\end{proposition}
The stop-gradient removes the target's own decay term $c^t_j$, which always dominates the drive.

\paragraph{Predictor scale and learning rate.}
$p_0$ enters through the window: a predictor initialized below $p_j^-$ never lets direction $j$ grow; one initialized far above $p_j^+$ loses $\tfrac{c_j}{2}p_0^2/(\alpha\lambda_P)$ of log-amplitude before entering the window. The predicted rank is therefore non-monotone in $p_0$.
The predictor learning rate enters through two terms of \eqref{eq:survival} with opposite effects: the drift rate $\alpha\lambda_P$ in $G_j$, which closes the window faster, and the nullcline $\lambda_Pp/(s_j-pc_j)$, which a faster predictor reaches at a lower amplitude when its decay rate is held fixed.
With a decay \emph{coefficient} $\lambda_P$ applied per unit of predictor learning rate, as in SGD or AdamW with a separate predictor learning rate, the drift rate $\alpha\lambda_P$ grows with $\alpha$ and $\hat R$ decreases with $\alpha$.
With a fixed absolute decay \emph{rate} $\eta_P=\alpha\lambda_P$, $G_j$ does not depend on $\alpha$ and the nullcline term $\tfrac12\log\big(\eta_Pp/(\alpha(s_j-pc_j))\big)$ decreases with it, so $\hat R$ increases with $\alpha$; this recovers the observation of \citet{tian2021understanding} that a fast predictor helps avoid collapse.
The sign of the learning-rate effect thus follows from the weight-decay convention; in both, a stronger predictor decay lowers $\hat R$.

\begin{corollary}[Predictor depth]
\label{cor:depth}
Let the predictor be a product of $L$ linear layers with balanced initialization, so that along direction $j$ its gain is $p_j=a_j^L$ and each factor obeys $\dot a_j=\alpha\big(a_j^{L-1}w_j^2(s_j-p_jc_j)-\lambda_Pa_j\big)$.
Near collapse $p_j(t)=p_0e^{-L\alpha\lambda_Pt}$, and \cref{prop:survival} holds with $\alpha\lambda_P$ replaced by $L\alpha\lambda_P$ in $G_j$ and $p_T$, and with nullcline term $\tfrac12\log\big(\lambda_Pp^{2/L-1}/(s_j-pc_j)\big)$.
\end{corollary}
At fixed end-to-end initial gain $p_0$, a deeper predictor closes every window $L$ times faster and so predicts lower rank; in the same numerical check the corollary agrees with integration in 149, 150 and 147 of 150 random settings for $L=1,2,3$ (\cref{app:survival}).

\paragraph{Predictor width.}
A predictor of rank $k$ (a bottleneck) has $p_j=0$ outside its range, where $r_j=-\lambda<0$: $\hat R\le k$.

\paragraph{Masking ratio.}
\begin{proposition}[Random masking of a stationary signal]
\label{prop:masking}
Let $x\in\R^D$ be zero-mean and stationary (circulant covariance with spectrum $\{S_k\}$ and variance $\sigma^2=\tfrac1D\sum_kS_k$), let each coordinate be visible in the context independently with probability $q=1-m$, and let the target be the masked coordinates. Then \cref{ass:codiag} holds in the Fourier basis with
\begin{equation}
s_k=q(1-q)(S_k-\sigma^2),\qquad c_k=q\big(qS_k+(1-q)\sigma^2\big).
\end{equation}
\end{proposition}
Two consequences.
First, only directions with above-average power, $S_k>\sigma^2$, can ever be driven ($s_k>0$): masked prediction discards the rest whatever the other hyperparameters.
Second, the existence condition \eqref{eq:fp} reads $q(1-q)^2(S_k-\sigma^2)^2>4\lambda\,(qS_k+(1-q)\sigma^2)$: the drive vanishes both without masking ($q\to1$) and without context ($q\to0$), and for high-power directions it increases with the masking ratio up to large $m$, in line with the large masking ratios used in practice \citep{assran2023ijepa,he2022mae}.

\paragraph{Target-encoder EMA.}
With $\dot{\Wt}=\kappa(W-\Wt)$, the linearization at the collapsed point grows if and only if $r_j(p)>0$ (\cref{app:proofs}): the EMA does not move the window, but it lowers the growth rate inside it and the predictor's signal $w_j\bar w_js_j$, so in \eqref{eq:survival} it acts like a smaller gain.
